;;; A boundary of M1e2 **W21**, banked by S1e2.2.21 (2026-11-05): a pair
;;; contradiction whose *negative* side is root's. No register row: the core
;;; the engine reports names the commitment.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program states `(not (p a))`. One candidate, `(q a)`: committed, it
;;; derives `(p a)` by `pos`, a pair whose positive side the commitment
;;; derives and whose negative side root states.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R is {∅}, and N is {(q a)}. The pair rests on `(p a)`, which rests on
;;; `(q a)`, and on `(not (p a))`, stated: the core is `(not (p a)) (q a)`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R as above, exit 0; and N's core `(q a)`, refuted by `(given)` and `pos`
;;; (`ein solve FILE --dump-answer DIR` at `cdcc3994`, release and debug):
;;; the positive side's frontier, which here is the commitment's, so the core
;;; names it, and it leaves out the stated `(not (p a))` the pair rests on
;;; too. W21's fix grows it to `(not (p a)) (q a)`; it names the commitment
;;; either way, which is why this file is a boundary and not a row.

(relation p T) (relation q T) (relation seed T)

(seed a)
(not (p a))

(hrule g () :match (seed ?x) :assert (q ?x))

(rule pos () :match (q ?x) :assert (p ?x))
